questions.description:
Your orchestrator stops a Node service by sending `SIGTERM`. After a grace period, it force-kills the process with `SIGKILL`.

```js
process.on('SIGTERM', () => {
  server.close(() => {   // stop taking new connections; fires once in-flight requests drain
    pool.end();          // release DB connections, flush logs, etc.
    process.exit(0);
  });
});
```

questions.explanation:
`server.close()` stops *accepting* new connections, but it doesn't close connections that are already open. With HTTP keep-alive, clients hold idle TCP connections open for reuse. The callback may not fire until each of those sockets times out, so shutdown looks like a hang.

Since **Node 18.2.0** you can close them yourself:

```js
process.on('SIGTERM', () => {
  server.close(() => process.exit(0));
  server.closeIdleConnections();   // drop idle keep-alive sockets right now
  // server.closeAllConnections();  // last-resort: also cut active ones
});
```

The other options are myths. `server.close()` works on plain HTTP. A ref'd `setInterval` does keep the event loop alive, but here the close callback isn't firing, which points at connections, not timers. And `unref()` has nothing to do with whether the close callback runs.

questions.description:
This handler runs on a single Node instance, with no `cluster` and no `worker_threads`:

```js
app.get('/report', (req, res) => {
  let total = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < 5e9; i++) total += i;  // several seconds of pure CPU
  res.json({ total });
});
```

More requests keep arriving at the **same instance**, both `/report` calls and requests to unrelated routes.
